{
  "workflowId": "300000331058004",
  "workflowCode": "ORA_AGENT_TEAM_PLANNER",
  "name": "Agent Team Planner",
  "description": "Builds planner proposals for an agent team: prepares node text, asks an LLM to produce three planner proposals (mapping phrases to node types and characteristics), then asks an LLM to choose a single best workflow proposal.",
  "specification": {
    "jsonSchemaName": "Workflow.spec",
    "jsonSchemaVersion": "1",
    "inputs": [],
    "outputSpecification": " ",
    "agentsValueMappings": [],
    "dataPipeline": {
      "pipelineNodes": [
        {
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Start of data pipeline",
            "name": "Start"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769645345702"
          },
          "code": "START",
          "id": "start",
          "inputs": [],
          "type": "START"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "description": "End of data pipeline",
            "name": "End"
          },
          "outcomes": {},
          "code": "END",
          "id": "end",
          "inputs": [],
          "type": "END"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "minimal"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "GPT-5 mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "make planner"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "MAKE_PLANNER",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1765848658665",
          "id": "1765846318558",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1765846318562_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Read {{$context.$nodes.SETNODE.$output.text}} in order to define the nodes and their variables. Your task is to make an ordered list of nodes in order to make a workflow based  {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}. Read the input to get a better context regarding which nodes to create, focus on the least amount of nodes\nYou will split each phrase, then decide if it should be  one of the following based on its description:\n - LLM: The Large Language Model node should be used for summarizing, compiling information from other nodes, taking decisions and and overall the role of an AI. Do not make concatenated LLM nodes, make only with the information\n- Business Object Function: This is the main node where  specific data, namely corporate and individual information that would be stored inside a Database is pulled from, it can either pull all entries or an specific entry based on its id. it is also used for adding an entry. a business object function can only get a single type of object per request but it can get one or multiple entries of the type of object. Drafts, examples, samples are not to be used for a Business Object Function\n- IF condition:  This special node depends if the  {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}} contains a boolean condition. only add this node if the {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}} has a condition.\n- Email:  This node is used only the workflow has to send information via email or an email address or contact an user, the subject should be the summarized result of the previous node. Only add this node if the user's input  explicitly contains instructions for sending an email or has an email address.\n- Human Approval: This node is the one that  makes the workflow interact with the user past the initial input, its usage is to make the user approve what other nodes make, it should be only used after an llm to make the change. Only add this node if the  {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}} says to validate with the user\n- File attachment: This node is required to read the file coming from the user\n- RAG Document tool:   The Retrieval-Augmented Generation  is used as a source of truth, mostly used for considering policies, rules and internal structure as well as how-to documentation. prioritize when the user's input has the context needing some policy, ruling.\n\n{\"nodes\":  [\n-\"node_type\": {\"BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION\",\"LLM\"\",\"EMAIL\",\"HUMAN APPROVAL\",\"FILE ATTACHMENT\", \"IF CONDITION\", \"LOOP\", \"RAG\"}\n- \"condition\": ONLY USED IF THE NODE TYPE IS AN IF CONDITION. this gives a boolean condition in order to branch based on the condition, this condition should always be boolean.it is obligatory for a IF CONDITION node_type to have a condition key. it must appear after the node_type\n- \"collection\": ONLY USED IF THE NODE TYPE IS A LOOP. this collection is an array which the LOOP will iterate over. it is obligatory for a LOOP node_type to have a collection key. this value must appear after the node_type\n- \"parent_node\": ONLY USED IF THE NODE TYPE IS A LOOP OR IF CONDITION. This value contains the name of the node where either the collection or condition is being fetched.\n- \"characteristic\":What the node should be doing. The characteristic for each node should be:\n\t- LLM: the  suggested prompt\n\t- Business object Function: proposed description of a Business Object Function, this description should contain some on the variables that are going to be sent, these variables should only be referenced on the input. If on the input was given a business object function, the characteristic must be the name as well as a simpler characteristic .  Keep the characteristic in one paragraph. \n\t- Email: the characteristic will have an object with the following format:\n\t{\n\t\t\"toList\": to be explicitly given by the user, otherwise the value should be \"this value must either be requested to user by a Human Approval node during the creator workflow, asked by the generated workflow or from a business object\",\n\t\t\"ccList\": to be explicitly given by the user, otherwise the value should be \"this value must either be requested to user by a Human Approval node during the creator workflow, asked by the generated workflow or from a business object\",\n\t\t\"sender\": to be explicitly given by the user, otherwise the value should be \"this value must either be requested to user by a Human Approval node during the creator workflow, asked by the generated workflow or from a business object\".,\n\t\t\"subject\": a summary of the body, starting with \"AUTOMATED EMAIL -\" then the subject of the email\n\t\t\"body\": the information that the workflow created and needed to be relayed.\n\t}\nthe object contains the toList, ccList, the sender, the subject and body of the email as keys. the sender, toList and ccList need to be explicitly given by the user, otherwise the value should be \"this value must either be requested to user by a Human Approval node during the creator workflow, asked by the generated workflow or from a business object\".\n\t- Human Approval: the messageTemplate to be displayed to the user for an approval\n\t- Document Processor: the name of the document that was given by the user\n\t- IF CONDITION: the characteristic will have an object with the following format:\n\t\t{\n\t\t\"true\": [nodes],\n\t\t\"false\":[nodes]\n\t\t}\n\tthe values of the nodes arrays will contain nested nodes.These nested nodes will follow the same structure as this node description. if a branch needs to stop the flow of the rest of the nodes, add the \"END\" string at the end of the array, only one branch can have an \"END\". The nested nodes can be of any type except IF condition. Only one \"true\" key and one \"false\" key can exist per object\n- LOOP: The characteristic will contain an array of nodes.These nested nodes will follow the same structure as this node description.  The nested nodes can be of any type except LOOP\n- RAG:  the characteristic will have an object with the following format:{\n\t\t\"question:\":it contains the question for the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Document,\n\t\t\"tool\": the description that fits the information regarding the question\n}\n- \"name\": the name will be based on the characteristic, along with the type of the node\n\n]}\n\nAdditional rules:\n- If the LLM node returns a json, do not recommend the inputs.\n- An LLM should only return a JSON if it has a Business object Function after\n- The Business Object Function characteristic can only have one verb. It cannot fetch and create at the same time\n- Make the least amount of nodes\n- The first node must be an LLM\n- Do not suggest any validation\n- There must be cohesion between the variables of the neighboring nodes\n- Keep the plan with at maximum 2 * the number of business function nodes\n- If there is none of the verbs on the user's query, regarding business objects, do not create a business object node\n- The nodes you are making are to build an agent team, do not add nodes referencing an agent team\n- Only add the if condition node if there are true and false branches, those two branches must always be included and have nodes\n- The if condition node must never be a last node\n- The If condition node must always have a condition value, use \"condition\" as the key\n- The LOOP node must always have a collection value, use \"collection\" as the key.\n- The LOOP and IF CONDITION  node must always have a parent_node value, those two node types are the ones that can have the parent_node. This parent_node must be the immediate previous node that either gives the collection or the condition\n- if there is a plural noun and a Business Object Function was chosen, first call a Business object function with all the values, then a LOOP to process each entry individually\n- Only one characteristic key per object should be made\n- The email node should be added at the end of any chain, be it on the LOOP, the IF CONDITION or the final node if the user's input mentioned an email\n- Whenever a RAG node is chosen, the same information must not be looked by a BUSINESS OBJECT NODE.\n-Drafts, samples, examples or tests must never be submitted to a business object function. \n- The output must not have any variables or values\n- The output must not include any characters that need to be escaped, like quotes, double quotes besides the keys of the objects"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n        \"nodes\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"properties\": {}\n            }\n        }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "set_node",
            "returnType": "object",
            "sourceCode": "let node_spec = {\"text\": \"\\nStart node\\nNode Properties\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is \\\"START\\\". (NOTE:- This node never shows up in expression tree)\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is START\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutput Specification\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node..Start node always represents the start of a pipeline.\\ninputs\\nNONE\\noutputSpecification\\nNONE\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n\\nSample json\\n\\n\\nLoop Node\\nDescription:\\nExecutes a set of nodes repeatedly for each item in a list or until a condition is met. It allows iteration over data collections or repeated tasks.\\nSample Use cases:\\nLoop through a list of employees to fetch their current salaries via a Business Object functio n. Iterate over a batch of documents and send each one to an LLM or document processor.\\nCycle through customer IDs in a CRM to retrieve their latest activity logs.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nThis is mainly used in expressions to refer to input/output etc.. LOOP NODE doesn't have inputs and doesn't have outputs..\\nSo this node should not showup in the expression tree.\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is LOOP\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node\\n3\\nloopType\\nstring\\nYes\\nRepresents if the loop has to be invoked in a sequence or in parallel. There are two values of this property.\\n1. SEQUENTIAL:- The entire pipeline of the loop is invoked sequenctially for each item in the array represented by \\\"collection\\\" property.\\n2. PARALLEL:- The entire pipeline of the loop is invoked in parallel for each item in the array represented by \\\"collection\\\" property.\\nThe entire pipeline has access to two expressions.\\n1) $currentItem\\n2) $currentItemIndex\\nThese expressions will have a value only if a pipeline is being executed inside a LOOP node.\\nIn the pipeline hierarchy, the bottom most loop node in the depth first search tree will determine the values of\\n$currentItem and $currentItemIndex values.\\n4\\ndataPipeli ne\\nobject\\nYes\\nRepresents a new child data pipeline which needs to be invoked. However, this pipeline doesn't have the following properties\\n1. triggers\\n2. variables\\n\\nThis object only has the following properties\\n1. rootNode\\n2. pipelineNodes\\ninputs\\nThe only input to the loop node is a collection of objects which can be represented using an expression.\\n\\noutputSpecification\\nNONE\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n\\nSample json\\n\\nRuntime Restrictions\\nThe loop node needs to have some restrictions in place so that it doesn't run for ever... (Since we take a collection, technically it will not run for ever..)..\\nSince workflows are long running jobs, apart from memory constraints , there should not be time limits..\\n\\nSince there can be heavy CPU and memory consumption, the existing capacity planning has to be revisited and updated (and necessary approvals have to be obtained)\\n\\nRun in parallel Node\\nDescription:\\nExecutes multiple branches of the workflow concurrently and converges back at a defined point. This is used to save time when tasks are independent.\\nSample Use cases:\\nFetch employee details, salary data, and performance records from different systems at the same time.\\nRun multiple LLM prompts in parallel (e.g., summarization, sentiment analysis, and keyword extraction on the same document). Query multiple vector databases simultaneously and merge the results.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nThis is mainly used in expressions to refer to input/output etc.. This node doesn't have inputs and doesn't have outputs..\\nSo this node should not showup in the expression tree.\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is PARALLEL\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can multiple outcomes.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n8\\nconvergen ceTargetId\\nstring\\nYes\\nThe id of the node at which all the branches merge\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node\\ninputs\\nNONE\\noutputSpecification\\nNONE\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n\\nSample json\\n\\nDesigntime Restrictions\\nFor 26A, lets only allow 10 branches maximum. This should be more than enough for most of the usecases.\\n\\nSwitch Node\\nDescription:\\nRoutes the workflow into different branches based on the value of a variable or expression. Each case represents a different execution path.\\nSample Use cases:\\nCustomer Support Bot\\ncase: \\\"billing\\\" Call the Billing Agent to fetch invoices. case: \\\"technical\\\" Trigger the Tech Support Workflow. case: \\\"account\\\" Run Account Management BO functions. default Route to General FAQ LLM node.\\nEmployee Onboarding Workflow\\ncase: \\\"full-time\\\" Run background check, assign equipment, provision email.\\ncase: \\\"contractor\\\" Provision limited access, skip payroll.\\ncase: \\\"intern\\\" Trigger mentorship matching workflow.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nThis is mainly used in expressions to refer to input/output etc.. This node doesn't have inputs and doesn't have outputs..\\nSo this node should not showup in the expression tree.\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is SWITCH\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can multiple outcomes.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n8\\nconvergen ceTargetId\\nString\\nYes\\nThe id of the node at which all the branches merge\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node\\ninputs\\nThe only input to the loop node is an expression which can get evaluated to a string.\\n\\n\\noutputSpecification\\nNONE\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n\\nSample json\\n\\nDesigntime Restrictions\\nFor 26A, lets only allow 10 branches maximum. This should be more than enough for most of the usecases. The case value cannot be \\\"success\\\" or \\\"failure\\\"\\nCode node\\nDescription:\\nThe CODE node allows users to write and execute custom JavaScript inside the workflow. It can transform inputs, perform calculations, enrich data, or implement conditional logic that isn't covered by other predefined nodes.\\nIt acts as an escape hatch: whenever the workflow builder doesn't have a specialized node for a use case, the CODE node lets you extend functionality without waiting for new features.\\n\\nThe maximum time this node can take is 5 seconds. Beyond that this node must timeout.\\nThe code node cannot use any of the input/output stream related API's..Even console log, print kind of API's are not allowed. Infinite loops are not allowed.\\nThe javascript code provided in this node can only expect to access the following three items.\\n$context\\n$currentItem\\n$currentItemIndex\\nThe code node cannot update the state of any variable/property...It can only return an object back.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nA unique code which is used to represent the outcome of the node\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is CODE\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node..Start node always represents the start of a pipeline.\\n3\\nsourceCode\\nstring\\nYes\\njavascript code with only basic computation capabilities.\\ninputs\\nNONE\\n\\noutputSpecification\\nThe output of a node can be a json object. So the admin can provide a specification which can be used by DT.\\n\\n\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n2\\nfailure\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a failure (like timeout, lengthy loops, huge strings etc)\\n\\nSample json\\n\\nRuntime Restrictions\\nThe following restrictions have to be place before code is run.\\n1. Allowed Operations:-\\na. JSON manipulation\\nb. Array/object processing methods\\nc. Math operations\\nd. String manipulations\\ne. Date utilities\\nf. Basic type checking\\n2. Limits:-\\na. 5 second timeout\\nb. 10000 maximum iterations\\nc. 50000 maximum length of output (50 KB of serialized output)\\nd. 2000 chars (i.e 2KB of code length)\\n3. Block all IO operations\\na. No console/print operations\\nb. Rest calls are not allowed\\nc. Filesystem access is not allowed\\n4. Block all imports of any libraries\\n5. No functions definitions allowed. So recurssions are not allowed.\\n6. Only straight-line procedural code is allowed.\\n7. The code cannot update the $context json object state\\n\\nDesigntime Restrictions\\nAs much as possible the code patterns have to be validate based on the above runtime restrictions.\\n\\n\\nHuman in the flow Node\\nDescription:\\nThe HUMAN APPROVAL node pauses the pipeline execution and routes the workflow output to a human reviewer (e.g., a manager, analyst, or subject matter expert). The reviewer can approve, reject, or sometimes provide feedback, after which the pipeline resumes along the appropriate path.\\nThis ensures critical checks are made by humans before the workflow continues, striking a balance between automation and oversight.\\nSample Use cases:\\nDocument Processing in HR\\nThe pipeline extracts employee data from uploaded forms.\\nA HUMAN APPROVAL node presents the parsed data to an HR staff member.\\nThe HR staff verifies the accuracy and approves before the data is written to the HCM system.\\nRecruitment Workflow\\nAI extracts candidate qualifications and scores them.\\nHUMAN APPROVAL node allows a recruiter to review the shortlist before scheduling interviews.\\nThis node cannot be added in a branch if the branch is inside a LOOP OR RUN_IN_PARALLEL node? (This will complicate the whole flow execution as the loop back node can be any node..)\\nIf we restrict like mentioned above, then runtime implementation should not be an issue.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nA unique code which is used to represent the outcome of the node\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is HUMAN\\n\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n8\\nemailAccou ntMappingR owId\\nString\\nconditionally required (if channelType is EMAIL)\\nThis id is used by the UI to select the rows in the emails related lov.\\n9\\nemailAccou ntId\\nString\\nconditionally required (if channelType is EMAIL)\\nThis is the actual email account id used to monitor the incoming approver's message. This is used by runtime\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node..Start node always represents the start of a pipeline.\\n3\\nchannelType\\nstring\\nYes\\nThere can be two values.\\nCHAT EMAIL\\n4\\nmaxIterations\\nnumber\\nYes\\nNumber of times the node can be processed if the response returned by the approver (human) is to request change again.\\n5\\nfeedbackEnable dFlag\\nboolean\\nNo\\nDefault value is \\\"true\\\".\\n6\\nloopBackNodeId\\nstring\\nConditional(only when feedbackEnabledFlag is true)\\nThe id of the node to be executed when the human wants to request changes.\\ninputs\\nThe human in the flow node can have different structures of inputs depending on the channelType There can be three templates in 26A.\\n1. messageTemplate\\n2. timeoutTemplate\\n3. maxIterationsExceededTemplate\\nAll the templates will capture a \\\"message\\\" which needs to be posted back to the HUMAN. If \\\"channelType\\\" is \\\"EMAIL\\\", then the templates can have \\\"emailHeaders\\\" property as well..\\n\\\"inputs\\\": [\\n{\\n\\\"name\\\": \\\"emailChannelInput\\\", \\\"type\\\" : \\\"object\\\",\\n\\\"value\\\":\\n{\\n\\\"timeout\\\": \\\"{{expression evaluating to a number..If invalid value, then the timeout\\ndefault value is 72 hours}}\\\",\\n\\\"messageTemplate\\\":{\\n              \\\"from\\\": \\\"{{expression which can evaluate to an emailid}}\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"string with expressions\\\",\\n\\\"emailHeaders\\\": {\\n\\\"to\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"cc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n                                    \\\"bcc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which have to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"subject\\\": \\\"{{expression representing a subject message}}\\\"\\n}\\n},\\n\\\"timeoutTemplate\\\":{\\n\\\"message\\\": \\\"string with expressions\\\", \\\"emailHeaders\\\": {\\n\\\"to\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"cc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n                                    \\\"bcc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which have to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"subject\\\": \\\"{{expression representing a subject message}}\\\"\\n}\\n},\\n\\\"maxIterationsExceededTemplate\\\":{ \\\"message\\\": \\\"string with expressions\\\", \\\"emailHeaders\\\": {\\n\\\"to\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"cc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which\\nhave to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n                                    \\\"bcc\\\": \\\"{{expression representing value a comma separated list of email id's which have to be used as to list}}\\\",\\n\\\"subject\\\": \\\"{{expression representing a subject message}}\\\"\\n}\\n}\\n}\\n},\\n{\\n     \\\"name\\\": \\\"chatChannelInput\\\", \\\"type\\\" : \\\"object\\\",\\n\\\"value\\\": {\\n                        \\\"requestChangeMessage\\\": \\\"{{expression evaluating a message to be shown when the request changes button is clicked}}\\\",\\n\\\"messageTemplate\\\":{\\n\\\"message\\\": \\\"string with expressions\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"maxIterationsExceededTemplate\\\":{ \\\"message\\\": \\\"string with expressions\\\"\\n}\\n}\\n}\\n]\\noutputSpecification\\nThe only output that can be returned is the action performed by the human workflow.\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n2\\nfailure\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a failure (like timeout, lengthy loops, huge strings etc)\\n\\nSample json\\n\\n\\nDesigntime Restrictions\\nHuman approver node cannot be added inside a loop/Run in parallel branches.\\n\\nRAG Document tool node\\nDescription:\\nThe RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) DOCUMENT TOOL node integrates with document tools defined in AI Studio to perform retrieval-augmented tasks. It typically combines document retrieval (from vector database) with LLM-powered generation.\\nThis node is useful when you want the AI to answer questions or perform tasks based on specific documents.\\nSample Use cases:\\nCustomer Support Knowledge Base\\nA customer asks, \\\"What is the refund policy for online orders?\\\"\\nThe RAG DOCUMENT TOOL node retrieves the refund policy section from company manuals using the document tool and passes this context to the LLM, which generates a precise, policy-compliant answer.\\nEmployee Onboarding Assistant\\nNew hires ask onboarding questions like, \\\"How many vacation days do I get in my first year?\\\"\\nThe node retrieves HR policy documents using the document tool and generates an accurate answer.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nA unique code which is used to represent the outcome of the node\\n\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is RAG_DOCUMENT_TOOL\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node..Start node always represents the start of a pipeline.\\n3\\ntoolCode\\nstring\\nYes\\nThe unique code of the tool.\\ninputs\\nOnly a natural language text is the input to this tool.\\nEven though \\\"inputs\\\" is an array, there will be only one item in the array.\\n\\n\\noutputSpecification\\n\\n\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n2\\nfailure\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a failure (like timeout, lengthy loops, huge strings etc)\\nSample json\\n\\nWorkflow node\\nDescription:\\nThe WORKFLOW node allows the current workflow to call another workflow. The called workflow can run independently and return results to the parent workflow. This promotes modularity, reusability, and cleaner design since commonly used flows don't need to be duplicated - they can be defined once and reused across many pipelines.\\nSample Use cases:\\nHR Automation\\nMain workflow = Onboard New Employee.\\nAt a certain step, it invokes a Background Check Workflow (reusable across all hiring pipelines). Once background check completes, control returns to the onboarding workflow.\\nCustomer Support Bot\\nMain workflow = Handle Customer Query.\\nIf the query requires escalation, the WORKFLOW node calls the Ticket Creation Workflow. That workflow logs the issue in the support system and returns a ticket ID back to the parent.\\nNode Properties\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\nComments\\n1\\nid\\nString\\nYes\\nThis represents an identifier of the node.\\n(The value of this property might be used as the value of \\\"rootNode\\\" property described above or can also be used when navigation has to happen within the pipeline\\nThe value of this property can only be a alpha numeric and must start with an alphabet.\\n2\\ncode\\nString\\nYes\\nA unique code which is used to represent the outcome of the node\\n3\\ntype\\nString\\nYes\\nThe value of this property for this node is WORKFLOW\\n4\\nmetadata\\nObject\\nOptional\\nEach functionality (i.e node type) might require some metadata specific to the functionality.\\nThis metadata needed to invoke the functionality is represented by this property. Look at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n5\\ninputs\\nArray< Object>\\noptional\\nEvery pipeline node (which represents an executable funtionality), might needs a set of inputs for the functionality to work . The definition of each input (like name, description etc) is part of this collection.\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n6\\noutputSpecif ication\\nString\\noptional\\nInstead of defining outputs, a json specification can be specified. (outputSpecification will take precedence when compared to outputs)\\n7\\noutcomes\\nObject\\noptional\\nIn the pipeline after processing a node the orchestrator logic has to know which pipeline node it has to process next. This information is capture by this \\\"outcomes\\\" property.\\nThe processing of a node can only have two outcomes. Either the processing was a success or the processing failed.\\nFor each outcome the end user might have configured what node of the pipeline has to be processed next. This information is captured using this property.\\n\\nLook at node specification section below to understand the object structure\\n\\nmetadata\\nThe following describes the metadata which is applicable to the business object node in the pipeline.\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nname\\nstring\\nYes\\nThis represents the name of the node which is only used by design time to show a meaningful name of the node.\\n2\\ndescription\\nstring\\nYes\\nDescription of the node..Start node always represents the start of a pipeline.\\n3\\nworkflowCode\\nstring\\nYes\\nThe unique code of the workflow whose latest published version has to be invoked.\\ninputs\\nThe workflow nodes webhook trigger type inputs needs to be captured as inputs\\n\\n\\n\\noutputSpecification\\nschema\\n{\\n\\\"$schema\\\": \\\"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n\\\"properties\\\": { \\\"input\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"output\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"error\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"null\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"followUpQuestions\\\": { \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\", \\\"items\\\": [\\n{\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n{\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n}\\n]\\n},\\n\\\"jobId\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"conversationId\\\": { \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"traceId\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"status\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"supportingChunks\\\": { \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n\\\"items\\\": {}\\n},\\n\\\"citations\\\": { \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n\\\"items\\\": {}\\n},\\n\\\"nodes\\\": {\\n\\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n\\\"items\\\": {}\\n},\\n\\\"waitingToolCode\\\": { \\\"type\\\": \\\"null\\\"\\n},\\n\\\"workflowCode\\\": {\\n\\n\\noutcomes\\nThe start node only has one outcome\\n\\nProperty Path\\nType\\nRequired\\nDescription\\n1\\nsuccess\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a success.\\n2\\nfailure\\nstring\\nOptional\\nThis property captures the next node to be processed when processing is a failure (like timeout, lengthy loops, huge strings etc)\\n\\nSample json\\n\\n\\nDesigntime Restrictions\\nA workflow having HUMAN approver node cannot be added inside a loop/Run in parallel branches.\\n\\n\\nOther Node changes\\nFrom 26A, all the nodes can have error outcome related metadata (except for START/END/ADD nodes)\\n\\nDesign time change\\nUI will provide an ability to specify which node in the workflow to navigate to when an error condition is encountered.\\n\\nRuntime change\\nIf an error condition is encountered, the following needs to be done.\\n1. set the status of the node to failure (so that the expression $context.$nodes.<nodecode>.$status will be failure)\\n2. If the node is one of the above nodes, if \\\"failure\\\" outcome is configured, follow to the next node as configured.\\n3. Only if the node doesn't have any \\\"failure\\\" outcome invoke the global error handler as configured at the workflow level.\\n\\nExpression changes\\nThe following are the new additions which have to be introduced from 26A.\\nChanges related to all nodes\\n$context.$nodes.<nodecode>.$status - This represents the status of a node execution. The possible runtime values are \\\"success\\\" or \\\"failure\\\". This is available for all the nodes.\\n$context.$nodes.<nodecode>.$error - This is an object which will be populated only when a node execution errors out. This is available for all the nodes.\\tThe sub properties of this object are\\n$context.$nodes.<nodecode>.$error.$info - The error information that got captured (like error message, trace etc)\\n$context.$nodes.<nodecode>.$error.$code - Any error code if any\\nChanges related to Human approver node\\n$context.$nodes.<human_node_code>.$actionPerformed - This represents the action human performed (like APPROVE/REJECT\\n/REQUEST_CHANGES)\\n$context.$nodes.<human_node_code>.$feedbackRecieved- This is the message that was recieved from human which can be used to regenerate new content.\\n$context.$nodes.<human_node_code>.$iterationsPerformed:- The number of times the human approver node got invoked before the \\\"$actionPerformed\\\" value changed to APPROVE/REJECT\\nChanges related to BO/External REST node\\n$context.$nodes.<bo_externalRest_node_code>.$responseHeaders- This is an object representing the http headers in the response received . This can be used like $context.$nodes.<bo_node_code>.$responseHeaders.<headerName> (So this is available only for bo and external rest nodes)\\nChanges related Loop REST node\\n$context.$nodes.<bo_externalRest_node_code>.$currentItem\\n$context.$nodes.<bo_externalRest_node_code>.$currentItemIndex\\n$currentItem - This represents the current object being processed in the current loop ..So its just a short curt to current loop nodes\\n$context.$nodes.<bo_externalRest_node_code>.$currentItem\\n$currentItemIndex - This represents the current object's index in the collection which is being processed by the current loop.\\tSo its just a\\nshortcut to current loop nodes\\n$context.$nodes.<bo_externalRest_node_code>.$currentItemIndex\\n\"};\nreturn node_spec;"
          },
